1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,140
Moving on to endocrine pancreas,

2
00:00:02,240 --> 00:00:04,480
recall that the pancreas is sort of two organs in one.

3
00:00:04,580 --> 00:00:08,320
There's an exocrine pancreas that produces the digestive enzymes

4
00:00:08,320 --> 00:00:10,540
that are then secreted into the small bowel.

5
00:00:11,100 --> 00:00:13,260
And then there's the endocrine pancreas,

6
00:00:13,340 --> 00:00:15,620
which is what we're going to discuss in this particular chapter.

7
00:00:16,300 --> 00:00:19,280
The endocrine pancreas is actually composed of clusters of cells

8
00:00:19,280 --> 00:00:20,720
called the islets of Langerhans.

9
00:00:21,160 --> 00:00:23,000
Each islet contains multiple cell types,

10
00:00:23,140 --> 00:00:25,940
and these cell types then produce one particular type of hormone.

11
00:00:26,360 --> 00:00:28,940
For example, insulin is secreted by the beta cells.

12
00:00:28,940 --> 00:00:31,380
Examiners like you to know that the beta cells

13
00:00:31,380 --> 00:00:33,100
lie in the center of the islets.

14
00:00:33,200 --> 00:00:34,960
That's an important thing that shows up on exams.

15
00:00:35,520 --> 00:00:38,200
Recall that insulin is the major anabolic hormone of the body.

16
00:00:38,440 --> 00:00:41,880
It upregulates GLUT4 receptors on skeletal muscle and adipose tissue.

17
00:00:42,520 --> 00:00:45,020
This then allows for glucose to enter into those tissues,

18
00:00:45,140 --> 00:00:46,680
which then lowers the serum glucose.

19
00:00:47,160 --> 00:00:49,120
When glucose enters into those tissues,

20
00:00:49,240 --> 00:00:51,020
it leads to increased glycogen synthesis,

21
00:00:51,240 --> 00:00:52,500
increased protein synthesis,

22
00:00:52,700 --> 00:00:55,680
and increased synthesis of fat or lipogenesis.

23
00:00:56,400 --> 00:00:58,760
Recall that insulin is opposed by glucagon,

24
00:00:58,760 --> 00:01:01,100
which is secreted by the alpha cells of the islets,

25
00:01:01,580 --> 00:01:03,800
and the glucagon increases blood sugar

26
00:01:03,800 --> 00:01:06,840
via breakdown of glycogen or glycogenolysis

27
00:01:06,840 --> 00:01:09,180
and via breakdown of fat or lipolysis.

28
00:01:09,860 --> 00:01:11,380
Here's a picture of an islet,

29
00:01:11,600 --> 00:01:13,200
and I put this picture here to remind you

30
00:01:13,200 --> 00:01:16,300
that the beta cells sit in the center of the islet.

31
00:01:16,800 --> 00:01:19,060
First disorder is type 1 diabetes mellitus.

32
00:01:19,200 --> 00:01:21,340
This is when you have an insulin deficiency,

33
00:01:21,880 --> 00:01:23,700
which then leads to a metabolic disorder

34
00:01:23,700 --> 00:01:25,580
characterized by hyperglycemia.

35
00:01:25,580 --> 00:01:28,680
So again, key problem is that you lose the ability to produce insulin.

36
00:01:29,140 --> 00:01:33,120
This occurs because you have an autoimmune destruction of the beta cells by T lymphocytes.

37
00:01:33,240 --> 00:01:34,900
It's a type 4 hypersensitivity.

38
00:01:35,460 --> 00:01:38,500
The characteristic finding will be inflammation of the islets.

39
00:01:38,660 --> 00:01:40,020
Board examiners like you to know that.

40
00:01:40,180 --> 00:01:41,920
You will see inflammation of the islets.

41
00:01:42,420 --> 00:01:45,580
It's associated with HLA-DR3 and HLA-DR4,

42
00:01:45,960 --> 00:01:49,240
and patients can have autoantibodies against insulin in their blood.

43
00:01:49,520 --> 00:01:54,260
These are often present and may be seen years before the actual clinical disease develops.

44
00:01:54,260 --> 00:01:56,320
Now, this will manifest in childhood.

45
00:01:56,540 --> 00:01:57,620
That's when it usually occurs,

46
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:00,480
and it manifests with features of insulin deficiency.

47
00:02:00,960 --> 00:02:02,420
So because there is insulin deficiency,

48
00:02:02,600 --> 00:02:04,340
the patients will have high serum glucose.

49
00:02:04,580 --> 00:02:09,540
Of course, a lack of insulin will lead to decreased uptake by skeletal muscle and fat.

50
00:02:10,080 --> 00:02:13,460
In addition, the patients will have weight loss and low muscle mass,

51
00:02:13,540 --> 00:02:16,200
and again, that's because they have unopposed glucagon,

52
00:02:16,480 --> 00:02:19,340
and the glucagon will result in gluconeogenesis,

53
00:02:19,540 --> 00:02:22,320
which requires amino acids, and so you'll break down muscle.

54
00:02:22,320 --> 00:02:24,980
Glycogenolysis, where you'll break down glycogen,

55
00:02:25,140 --> 00:02:27,800
and lipolysis, where you will break down fat.

56
00:02:28,320 --> 00:02:30,960
They can also have polyphagia, which means that they eat a lot.

57
00:02:31,240 --> 00:02:34,440
In addition, the patients will have polyuria with glucose in the urine,

58
00:02:34,780 --> 00:02:36,420
and will also be drinking a lot.

59
00:02:36,900 --> 00:02:39,420
Now, the idea here is that you'll have a high glucose in the blood,

60
00:02:39,660 --> 00:02:42,160
and that will result in a high filtration by the kidney.

61
00:02:42,940 --> 00:02:46,320
The hyperglycemia will exceed the renal ability to resorb sugar,

62
00:02:46,320 --> 00:02:48,500
and you'll start losing sugar in the urine.

63
00:02:48,980 --> 00:02:52,080
That's an osmotically active substance, which will also draw water with it,

64
00:02:52,320 --> 00:02:55,580
and so again, you'll have polyuria with glucose in the urine,

65
00:02:55,680 --> 00:02:57,900
and polydipsia, where the patients will be drinking a lot.

66
00:02:58,180 --> 00:03:00,060
And the treatment is lifelong insulin.

67
00:03:00,580 --> 00:03:03,780
One of the feared complications, and one of the classic mechanisms

68
00:03:03,780 --> 00:03:08,680
by which type 1 diabetes can present, is diabetic ketoacidosis.

69
00:03:09,280 --> 00:03:12,280
This is characterized by excessive ketoacids within the serum,

70
00:03:12,420 --> 00:03:14,680
hence the term diabetic ketoacidosis.

71
00:03:15,060 --> 00:03:17,720
It often arises with stress, for example, infection,

72
00:03:18,140 --> 00:03:21,240
and the idea here is that when you have stress, you get release of epinephrine,

73
00:03:21,540 --> 00:03:23,700
and epinephrine increases glucagon.

74
00:03:24,020 --> 00:03:26,860
Now, glucagon, of course, will increase lipolysis,

75
00:03:27,240 --> 00:03:31,760
and when you increase lipolysis, that's going to result in increased free fatty acids.

76
00:03:32,260 --> 00:03:35,760
Those free fatty acids will go to the liver and be converted to ketone bodies,

77
00:03:35,760 --> 00:03:41,480
and the ketone bodies, for example, are beta-hydroxybutyric acid and acetoacetic acid.

78
00:03:42,040 --> 00:03:45,880
Of course, these acids would then be the hallmark of diabetic ketoacidosis.

79
00:03:46,240 --> 00:03:48,180
Clinically, the patients will have a very high sugar,

80
00:03:48,320 --> 00:03:54,040
and that's because the glucagon is also inducing gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis,

81
00:03:54,160 --> 00:03:55,540
and so the sugar will be very high.

82
00:03:55,960 --> 00:03:58,660
They'll have an anion-gap metabolic acidosis,

83
00:03:58,720 --> 00:04:01,860
and that is because they've got ketoacids floating around in the blood.

84
00:04:02,440 --> 00:04:05,120
They'll have hyperkalemia, and this can happen for a few different reasons.

85
00:04:05,120 --> 00:04:09,160
Number one, insulin is useful for driving potassium into cells,

86
00:04:09,260 --> 00:04:12,760
and so if they don't have insulin, the potassium will be outside of the cell,

87
00:04:12,840 --> 00:04:13,920
giving them a hyperkalemia.

88
00:04:14,400 --> 00:04:16,340
And number two, when they get an acidosis,

89
00:04:16,420 --> 00:04:20,380
one of the buffering mechanisms is to put hydrogen into the cell and bring potassium out,

90
00:04:20,760 --> 00:04:23,040
and so they'll have a high potassium within their serum.

91
00:04:23,320 --> 00:04:26,760
Now, it's important to note that much of this potassium will actually be lost in the urine,

92
00:04:27,240 --> 00:04:29,900
and so over time, although the potassium is high in the blood,

93
00:04:30,200 --> 00:04:32,280
they're actually depleting the body of potassium,

94
00:04:32,280 --> 00:04:36,380
and so overall, the potassium is low in the body because it's being lost in the urine.

95
00:04:36,940 --> 00:04:39,280
In addition, the patients will have cost-mall respirations,

96
00:04:39,380 --> 00:04:42,140
and that's because they're trying to blow off this acidosis.

97
00:04:42,220 --> 00:04:47,720
They'll have dehydration because the hyperglycemia is leading to a massive diuresis,

98
00:04:48,100 --> 00:04:53,280
and they can have a fruity-smelling breath due to the presence of acetone within their serum.

99
00:04:53,720 --> 00:04:55,400
The treatment involves giving fluids.

100
00:04:55,540 --> 00:05:00,520
The patients are often dehydrated because of the diuretic effect of having so much sugar in the urine.

101
00:05:00,520 --> 00:05:04,860
In addition, insulin is given to counter the effects of glucagon and to replace insulin.

102
00:05:05,340 --> 00:05:08,500
In addition, you'll have to replace electrolytes, in particular potassium.

103
00:05:09,180 --> 00:05:12,940
Recall that although the patients are hyperkalemic, they've lost a lot of potassium in the urine.

104
00:05:13,260 --> 00:05:16,060
When you give insulin, that's going to force potassium into the cells,

105
00:05:16,160 --> 00:05:20,200
and also when you reverse the acidosis, potassium will also go back into the cells,

106
00:05:20,680 --> 00:05:22,520
and so these patients can quickly become hypokalemic,

107
00:05:23,660 --> 00:05:27,620
and so you need to replace potassium while all of this is going on.

108
00:05:27,620 --> 00:05:34,740
Now, in type 2 diabetes, you have end-organ resistance to insulin that leads to hyperglycemia.

109
00:05:35,060 --> 00:05:37,400
This is the most common type of diabetes.

110
00:05:37,700 --> 00:05:38,820
Its incidence is rising.

111
00:05:39,360 --> 00:05:42,480
The classic patient is a middle-aged obese adult.

112
00:05:42,920 --> 00:05:47,980
Obesity leads to a decreased number of insulin receptors on the skeletal muscle and the adipose tissue.

113
00:05:48,320 --> 00:05:50,360
That's very high yield for board examinations.

114
00:05:50,580 --> 00:05:53,200
They like you to know the mechanism of insulin resistance,

115
00:05:53,400 --> 00:05:55,880
and it is due to a decreased number of insulin receptors.

116
00:05:55,880 --> 00:05:59,820
There's a very strong genetic predisposition for type 2 diabetes.

117
00:05:59,980 --> 00:06:05,300
In fact, the genetic predisposition for type 2 diabetes is stronger than it is for type 1 diabetes,

118
00:06:05,440 --> 00:06:07,580
and that's also a high yield board association.

119
00:06:08,400 --> 00:06:11,820
In these particular patients, early on, the insulin levels are increased,

120
00:06:11,960 --> 00:06:17,180
and that's because there's an insulin resistance, and so the pancreas and so the islets will produce excess insulin.

121
00:06:17,540 --> 00:06:21,180
However, later there will be insulin deficiency as you get beta cell exhaustion.

122
00:06:21,180 --> 00:06:27,840
One of the interesting high yield points that you'll see on histology is that the islets will become full of amyloid,

123
00:06:28,180 --> 00:06:31,720
and so you can clearly see that there's all this amyloid within this islet here,

124
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:34,560
and that's very high yield for the purposes of exams.

125
00:06:35,060 --> 00:06:38,000
Now, clinically, these patients will have polyuria and polydipsia.

126
00:06:38,360 --> 00:06:42,280
At the same time, they'll have hyperglycemia, but often it's clinically silent,

127
00:06:42,280 --> 00:06:47,380
and in fact, many patients with type 2 diabetes don't even know that they have the disease.

128
00:06:48,040 --> 00:06:51,000
The diagnosis is made by detecting a high blood sugar.

129
00:06:51,340 --> 00:06:54,880
You can do so by finding a random blood glucose greater than 200,

130
00:06:55,480 --> 00:07:00,260
by measuring a fasting blood glucose, which should be greater than 126,

131
00:07:00,680 --> 00:07:04,660
or by doing a glucose tolerance test where you give the patients a specific amount of glucose

132
00:07:04,660 --> 00:07:07,840
and then measure the glucose after a few hours,

133
00:07:08,300 --> 00:07:11,600
and the presence of greater than 200 milligrams per deciliter of glucose

134
00:07:11,600 --> 00:07:16,100
two hours after the glucose load is also sufficient to make a diagnosis of diabetes.

135
00:07:16,500 --> 00:07:19,000
Treatment involves weight loss by a diet and exercise,

136
00:07:19,540 --> 00:07:21,200
and in fact, this is the first-line treatment.

137
00:07:21,680 --> 00:07:24,440
You could give drug therapy to counter the insulin resistance,

138
00:07:24,880 --> 00:07:29,000
and finally, in the end stage of the disease, the patients do become insulin-dependent.

139
00:07:29,320 --> 00:07:31,400
One of the key complications in type 2 diabetes

140
00:07:31,400 --> 00:07:34,060
is the risk for hyperosmolar non-ketotic coma.

141
00:07:34,560 --> 00:07:37,480
This is when the patients get very high blood glucose levels,

142
00:07:37,780 --> 00:07:40,340
which then leads to a life-threatening diuresis.

143
00:07:40,400 --> 00:07:42,800
So again, the high glucose will get filtered by the kidney,

144
00:07:43,280 --> 00:07:45,440
and the glucose is an osmotically active agent,

145
00:07:45,540 --> 00:07:47,960
which will then result in a massive diuresis.

146
00:07:48,600 --> 00:07:50,800
Now, this would then result in hypotension and coma,

147
00:07:51,120 --> 00:07:53,680
and in particular, it's important to note that ketones are absent.

148
00:07:53,840 --> 00:07:57,160
In this particular case, the patients do have small amounts of circulating insulin,

149
00:07:57,160 --> 00:08:01,520
and that insulin is sufficient enough to counter the effects of glucagon,

150
00:08:01,600 --> 00:08:03,120
and so ketones are usually absent.

151
00:08:03,320 --> 00:08:04,940
Again, going back to the term here,

152
00:08:05,240 --> 00:08:09,160
hyperosmolar, high blood sugar, non-ketotic, you don't have ketones, coma.

153
00:08:09,280 --> 00:08:12,440
You get life-threatening diuresis, which could put the patient into coma.

154
00:08:12,720 --> 00:08:15,460
Now, the last discussion, as far as diabetes is concerned,

155
00:08:15,700 --> 00:08:17,600
is to cover the complications of diabetes.

156
00:08:18,080 --> 00:08:19,780
And so basically, what I would like to remind you

157
00:08:19,780 --> 00:08:23,520
is that there are two major mechanisms by which you get complications in diabetes.

158
00:08:23,520 --> 00:08:28,060
The first is non-enzymatic glycosylation of vascular basement membrane.

159
00:08:28,360 --> 00:08:30,340
Non-enzymatic means you don't need an enzyme,

160
00:08:30,440 --> 00:08:33,260
and glycosylation means that you're sticking sugar onto something.

161
00:08:33,700 --> 00:08:36,680
And so when you stick sugar onto the vascular basement membrane,

162
00:08:36,920 --> 00:08:38,580
it's going to result in certain complications.

163
00:08:39,240 --> 00:08:41,720
When this occurs on the large and medium-sized vessels,

164
00:08:42,020 --> 00:08:44,120
you're going to increase the risk for atherosclerosis.

165
00:08:44,520 --> 00:08:47,140
And this then leads to many of the complications of diabetes.

166
00:08:47,280 --> 00:08:50,120
For example, cardiovascular disease is the leading cause of death,

167
00:08:50,120 --> 00:08:53,120
and cardiovascular disease is caused by atherosclerosis.

168
00:08:53,680 --> 00:08:58,820
Peripheral vascular disease is a leading cause of non-traumatic amputations in diabetic patients.

169
00:08:59,200 --> 00:09:02,400
And so atherosclerosis and all its related complications will occur.

170
00:09:02,940 --> 00:09:07,080
In addition, diabetics also get non-enzymatic glycosylation of their small blood vessels.

171
00:09:07,500 --> 00:09:10,080
That then leads to highline arteriolosclerosis,

172
00:09:10,200 --> 00:09:12,900
and we covered this in detail in the vascular chapter.

173
00:09:13,620 --> 00:09:17,160
Now, the classic location for highline arteriolosclerosis would be in the kidney.

174
00:09:17,160 --> 00:09:21,080
And recall that if this is the afferent arteriole, and this is the glomerulus,

175
00:09:21,180 --> 00:09:24,280
and this is the efferent arteriole, a couple of things can happen.

176
00:09:24,380 --> 00:09:28,260
Number one, you're going to get preferential involvement of the efferent arteriole.

177
00:09:28,560 --> 00:09:30,960
That's going to increase the pressure within the glomerulus,

178
00:09:31,060 --> 00:09:32,680
creating a hyperfiltration injury.

179
00:09:33,040 --> 00:09:35,460
The initial phase will be that you'll get microalbuminuria.

180
00:09:35,460 --> 00:09:40,880
Eventually, you'll get sclerosis of the mesangium, resulting in Kimmel-Steel-Wilson nodules,

181
00:09:41,180 --> 00:09:42,860
which will then result in nephrotic syndrome.

182
00:09:43,180 --> 00:09:48,040
So one complication of the highline arteriolosclerosis within the kidney will be nephrotic syndrome.

183
00:09:48,460 --> 00:09:53,340
Another complication will be that you will get some arteriolosclerosis of the afferent arteriole.

184
00:09:53,640 --> 00:10:00,260
This will then decrease the blood supply to the glomerulus and can result in diffuse sclerosis of the glomerulus,

185
00:10:00,660 --> 00:10:04,040
which would then, of course, also result in chronic renal failure.

186
00:10:04,040 --> 00:10:07,860
And so these patients can develop chronic renal failure over time as well.

187
00:10:08,260 --> 00:10:14,180
Finally, the third complication of non-enzymatic glicosylation is that you will have non-enzymatic glicosylation of hemoglobin.

188
00:10:14,540 --> 00:10:19,800
And when you get non-enzymatic glicosylation of hemoglobin, that produces a special hemoglobin called hemoglobin A1c.

189
00:10:20,240 --> 00:10:24,100
And it's important to note that hemoglobin A1c is a marker of glycemic control,

190
00:10:24,220 --> 00:10:27,320
because remember that the red blood cells, they last 120 days.

191
00:10:27,380 --> 00:10:33,960
And so you can get an idea of how well the sugar has been controlled over a period of time by looking at hemoglobin A1c.

192
00:10:34,480 --> 00:10:37,220
Here's a picture of highline arteriolosclerosis.

193
00:10:37,420 --> 00:10:44,300
Remember that highline arteriolosclerosis refers to the fact that you get pink material within the walls of small blood vessels,

194
00:10:44,440 --> 00:10:45,640
hence the term arteriolo.

195
00:10:45,960 --> 00:10:52,240
And this then decreases the luminal caliber, resulting in the complications that we've already described.

196
00:10:52,500 --> 00:10:55,220
Here's a picture of a kidney with glomerulosclerosis.

197
00:10:55,220 --> 00:10:59,260
The idea here is that you've got a shrunken kidney that appears scarred on its surface.

198
00:10:59,600 --> 00:11:06,540
And the reason it appears scarred on its surface is because you've got all that sclerosis going on in the glomeruli that I've already described.

199
00:11:06,960 --> 00:11:11,140
Again, this would then lead to chronic renal failure that is classically seen in diabetics.

200
00:11:12,080 --> 00:11:16,280
The second complication of having a high sugar is that you'll get asthmatic damage in certain cells.

201
00:11:16,680 --> 00:11:21,500
Now it's important to note that there are some cells in the body that will take up sugar without the need for insulin.

202
00:11:21,500 --> 00:11:25,320
For example, the Schwann cells have the ability to take up sugar without insulin.

203
00:11:26,020 --> 00:11:29,280
And so if you've got a high blood sugar, you're going to get high sugar within the Schwann cells.

204
00:11:29,720 --> 00:11:35,040
When you have high glucose within the Schwann cells, aldose reductase will convert the glucose to sorbitol.

205
00:11:35,320 --> 00:11:37,160
And sorbitol will lead to osmotic damage.

206
00:11:37,340 --> 00:11:38,740
This is very high yield.

207
00:11:39,260 --> 00:11:44,800
Now when you cause osmotic damage of the Schwann cells, recall that the Schwann cells, they myelinate the peripheral nervous system.

208
00:11:45,260 --> 00:11:48,620
And so that's going to result in the neuropathy that's seen in diabetics.

209
00:11:48,620 --> 00:11:52,900
In addition, the pericytes of the retinal blood vessels can take up sugar without insulin.

210
00:11:53,360 --> 00:11:57,920
And so let's remind ourselves that if this is the small blood vessel within the retina,

211
00:11:58,240 --> 00:12:01,520
and you've got the endothelial cells here, we're just talking about the capillaries.

212
00:12:01,660 --> 00:12:03,940
Again, endothelial cells sitting on basement membrane.

213
00:12:04,360 --> 00:12:09,600
Remember that underneath the endothelial cells in the microvasculature, in the capillaries, for example, in the venules,

214
00:12:09,980 --> 00:12:12,120
you've got these small cells called pericytes.

215
00:12:12,520 --> 00:12:14,660
And so here's an example of the pericytes here.

216
00:12:14,660 --> 00:12:18,640
What happens with a high sugar is that sugar goes into these pericytes.

217
00:12:18,840 --> 00:12:21,680
Aldose reductase converts the sugar to sorbitol.

218
00:12:22,060 --> 00:12:24,780
That then causes osmotic damage, and the pericytes die.

219
00:12:25,180 --> 00:12:30,460
When the pericytes die, you will then weaken this blood vessel wall, which will then result in an aneurysm.

220
00:12:30,840 --> 00:12:33,140
And so you can get aneurysms within the retina.

221
00:12:33,440 --> 00:12:37,320
These aneurysms can rupture, and then you get hemorrhage, and that then causes blindness.

222
00:12:37,820 --> 00:12:40,260
And recall that diabetes is an important cause of blindness.

223
00:12:40,260 --> 00:12:43,860
And finally, the lens can take a glucose even without insulin.

224
00:12:44,260 --> 00:12:50,580
And so the high glucose will go into the lens, will be converted to sorbitol by aldose reductase, again, high yield.

225
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:55,300
And that will then result in cataracts, and so that's also a complication seen in diabetics.

226
00:12:55,760 --> 00:12:59,720
Moving on to pancreatic endocrine tumors, these are tumors of the islet cells.

227
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:02,200
They're often a component of MEN1.

228
00:13:02,400 --> 00:13:08,000
MEN, again, is multiple endocrine neoplasia, where you get neoplasia of multiple endocrine organs.

229
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:16,900
In MEN1, the classic finding would be parathyroid hyperplasia, pituitary adenoma, and then a pancreatic endocrine tumor.

230
00:13:18,420 --> 00:13:21,740
Insulinoma is when you have a tumor that produces insulin.

231
00:13:22,180 --> 00:13:27,480
You get episodic hypoglycemia with mental status changes that are relieved by glucose.

232
00:13:28,080 --> 00:13:30,900
So the glucose would be down because of the insulinoma.

233
00:13:31,140 --> 00:13:32,240
The insulin would be up.

234
00:13:32,460 --> 00:13:35,460
And very important, very high yield, the C-peptide would also be up.

235
00:13:35,460 --> 00:13:41,200
Recall that when the body produces insulin, it also produces an additional protein with it called C-peptide.

236
00:13:41,620 --> 00:13:47,800
And so if you have a high insulin with a high C-peptide, it indicates that you're making the insulin within the human body,

237
00:13:47,880 --> 00:13:50,160
and hence you probably have an insulinoma.

238
00:13:50,700 --> 00:13:53,440
However, if the patient has a high insulin with no C-peptide,

239
00:13:53,520 --> 00:13:56,520
it means that they perhaps may be injecting themselves with insulin,

240
00:13:56,660 --> 00:13:59,780
for example, to seek the attention of medical professionals.

241
00:13:59,780 --> 00:14:02,680
Gastrinoma is a tumor that produces gastrin.

242
00:14:02,760 --> 00:14:08,940
Recall that gastrin goes to the parietal cells of the stomach and then causes the parietal cells to produce acid via their proton pumps.

243
00:14:09,560 --> 00:14:14,080
So if you have a gastrinoma, you're going to get excess production of acid by the parietal cells,

244
00:14:14,220 --> 00:14:17,520
which will then result in treatment-resistant peptic ulcers.

245
00:14:17,880 --> 00:14:19,760
That's called Zolliger-Ellison syndrome.

246
00:14:20,320 --> 00:14:24,240
These ulcers can be multiple, and they can extend into the jejunum.

247
00:14:24,240 --> 00:14:27,040
And so these are some of the classic features of a gastrinoma.

248
00:14:27,680 --> 00:14:31,280
A somatostatinoma is when you have a tumor that produces somatostatin.

249
00:14:31,920 --> 00:14:37,340
Somatostatin inhibits gastrin, and so the patients will have low acid production by the stomach, or achlorhydria.

250
00:14:38,040 --> 00:14:40,440
Somatostatin also inhibits cholecystokinin.

251
00:14:40,820 --> 00:14:45,140
Recall that cholecystokinin, or CCK, is important for contraction of the gallbladder,

252
00:14:45,460 --> 00:14:48,660
and so when you have a somatostatinoma, you won't have contraction of the gallbladder.

253
00:14:48,660 --> 00:14:54,100
And lack of contraction of the gallbladder will increase the risk for gallstones and for steatorrhea.

254
00:14:54,260 --> 00:15:00,000
Of course, if you're not putting the bile into the small bowel, you won't be able to digest fat very well,

255
00:15:00,260 --> 00:15:01,920
and so the patients will get steatorrhea.

256
00:15:02,480 --> 00:15:06,840
VIP-oma is a tumor that produces VIP, or vasoactive intestinal peptide.

257
00:15:07,220 --> 00:15:09,660
This will result in a watery diarrhea, achlorhydria.

258
00:15:10,500 --> 00:15:14,840
Remember that VIP inhibits gastric acid secretion, and hypokalemia.

259
00:15:15,340 --> 00:15:17,680
And so these are the key findings in VIP-oma.

260
00:15:17,680 --> 00:15:21,040
And that concludes our section on the endocrine pancreas.

